# Development Standards

These are strict rules that apply to ALL changes in the Meridian codebase. Violations cause regressions — enforce them without exception.

## Open Source First — Always Use the Best Publicly Available Tools

**Always build the Meridian engine from the latest, publicly available open-source components.** Never pin to old versions without a documented, tested regression in the newer version.

| Principle | Detail |
|-----------|--------|
| **Latest stable** | Use the latest stable release of every component. Version tables in this doc are regularly updated. |
| **Open source preferred** | Wine (LGPL), DXMT (Apache), DXVK (zlib), MoltenVK (Apache) are all open source. GPTK's D3DMetal.framework is Apple-proprietary but freely distributed by Apple for exactly this use case. |
| **CX Wine components are LGPL** | CX Wine (wine64, wineserver, DLLs, d3d12.dll) is LGPL 2.1 — legally redistributable. `release-engine.sh` sources Wine binary + DLLs + DXMT + DXVK + GPTK from CrossOver Preview. End users never need CrossOver installed — everything ships self-contained. |
| **Document everything learned** | Whenever CrossOver or any reference implementation reveals a technique, it goes straight into `engine-research-findings.mdc` so every future session builds on it rather than re-deriving it. |

## Steam UI Is Never Shown — Fundamental Design Principle

**Meridian has its own macOS UI for everything. The Windows Steam client is a hidden backend — its windows must NEVER surface to the user.**

The `SteamWindowSuppressor` uses the macOS Accessibility API to immediately hide any Wine window the moment it appears. This must be running whenever Wine processes are alive.

### The Only Legitimate Steam Window

The bootstrap phase (first launch, `steamui.dll` absent) is the one and only exception: Steam shows its update UI while downloading its ~150MB client. Meridian shows a progress screen during this, and it is expected. Everything else is a bug.

### What Causes Unexpected Steam UI (and the Fix)

| Symptom | Root Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Steam sign-in screen after wineboot | `wineboot --update` runs `HKCU\...\Run` entries; Steam is registered there | `refreshSystemDLLs()` deletes the Steam Run key before wineboot (CLI-verified April 2026) |
| Steam windows visible during bootstrap | `SteamWindowSuppressor` only engages after all prefix operations complete (step 6) | By design — wineboot/SteamSetup dialogs must render to complete. Steam autorun is handled by deleting the Run key. |
| Steam windows after Meridian crash | Meridian crashes without cleanup → Wine processes orphaned → suppressor gone | `TerminationCleanup` kills by Windows process name patterns (post-argv[0]-replacement) |
| Steam sign-in with `-silent` flag | Steam started but has no valid ConnectCache session | `SteamSessionBridge.prepare()` must run before any steam.exe start; check that session sync ran |

### The SteamWindowSuppressor Engagement Sequence

```
1. Cleanup kills previous session's Wine processes
2. wineboot / SteamSetup run (suppressor NOT engaged — their own dialogs must render)
3. Steam bootstrap downloads steamui.dll (suppressor NOT engaged — UI is intentional)
4. Session sync (SteamSessionBridge.prepare)
5. windowSuppressor?.beginSession()  ← suppressor engages HERE (step 6 in BootstrapManager)
6. SteamCMD warmup runs suppressed
7. DRM-game steam.exe starts — suppressor already active
```

**Never call `windowSuppressor?.beginSession()` before step 5.** The earlier phases need their Wine UI.

